@@s008 Functions and composition
@@s009 An ML model is a function, and almost always a chain of functions. Here: what the word function means, how to read the graph, the three types of function that the slides distinguish, how functions are composed and decomposed, and why it matters. Exercises 5 and 6 of week 1 (Functions and Function Composition, Grasple).
@@s010 What a function is
@@s011 A <b>function</b> is a rule that associates with each <b>input</b> <b>one single</b> result. It is written with a letter (usually f) followed by the input in brackets: <span class="m">f(x) = 2x + 1</span> means "the function f takes a number x, doubles it and adds 1".
@@s012 The letter <b>x</b> is a placeholder: it has no value of its own. <b>Evaluating</b> the function at a number means substituting that number for x everywhere it appears. f(3) = 2·3 + 1 = 7 is read "f of 3 is 7".
@@s013 The set of admitted inputs is called the <b>domain</b>; the set where the results fall is the <b>codomain</b>. The notation <span class="m">f: ℝ → ℝ</span> says exactly this: f takes a real number and returns a real number.
@@s014 The rule "one input, one single output" is what makes a function useful as a model: given the same x, the prediction must always be the same.
@@s015 The function as a machine
@@s016 The graph of a function
@@s017 The <b>graph</b> of f is the set of all the points (x, f(x)): for every input on the horizontal axis you go up to the height of the result. It serves to see at a glance how the function behaves: where it rises, where it falls, where it is zero.
@@s018 Reading it the other way round is a quick way to check whether a curve is the graph of a function: the <b>vertical line test</b>. A vertical line fixes an x; if it touches the curve at more than one point, that x would have more results, and the curve is not a function of x.
@@s019 In the course the graphs serve mostly for regression models: the data are points, the model is a curve, and the error of each point is the vertical distance between the point and the curve.
@@s020 Parabola yes, circle no
@@s021 One variable, several variables, vector values
@@s022 Slide 25 of lecture 1 distinguishes three types of function, and the course uses them all.
@@s023 <b>Single-valued function of one variable</b> (<i>single valued function</i>), <span class="m">f: ℝ → ℝ</span>: a number goes in, a number comes out. The 1D linear model is of this type.
@@s024 <b>Of several variables</b> (<i>multivariate function</i>), <span class="m">f: ℝⁿ → ℝ</span>: a <b>vector</b> of n numbers goes in, a number comes out. For example <span class="m">f(x₁, x₂) = 2x₁ + 3x₂</span>. It is the form of regression with several features: the graph is no longer a curve, but a plane or a hyperplane (<a href="w02-iperpiani.html">lines, planes, hyperplanes</a>).
@@s025 <b>With vector values</b> (<i>multivariate vector function</i>), <span class="m">f: ℝⁿ → ℝᵐ</span>: a vector goes in, another vector comes out. For example <span class="m">f(x₁, x₂) = (x₁ + x₂, x₁ − x₂)</span>. The geometric transformations of week 4 and the model of Assignment 1, which predicts two screen coordinates from the pupil position, are of this type.
@@s026 ℝ → ℝ, ℝ² → ℝ, ℝ² → ℝ²
@@s027 Composing two functions
@@s028 <b>Composing</b> means putting two functions in a row: the output of the first becomes the input of the second. It is written <span class="m">f(g(x))</span>, or <span class="m">(f ∘ g)(x)</span>, and the symbol ∘ is read "after": f after g.
@@s029 It is computed <b>from inside to outside</b>: first the innermost function (g), then the one that wraps it (f). With g(x) = x + 1 and f(x) = x²: f(g(2)) = f(3) = 9.
@@s030 The order <b>matters</b>. g(f(2)) = g(4) = 5, a different result. In general f(g(x)) ≠ g(f(x)): composition is not commutative. This fact returns identically with matrices, where "apply R first and then S" gives a different result from "S first and then R" (<a href="w04-trasformazioni.html">transformations</a>).
@@s031 f(g(2)) and g(f(2))
@@s032 Decomposing a function
@@s033 The exercise in reverse: given a complicated function, recognise the simple functions it is made of. In <span class="m">h(x) = (3x − 1)²</span> there are two operations: first 3x − 1 is computed, then it is squared.
@@s034 The <b>inner</b> function is <span class="m">g(x) = 3x − 1</span>, the <b>outer</b> one is <span class="m">f(u) = u²</span>. The letter u is just a name for the input of f, to remind you that it is not x but the result of g. So h = f ∘ g.
@@s035 How to find the inner function: look at what the <b>last</b> operation would be if you computed h with a number. That is the outer function; everything underneath is the inner function. Decomposing in this way is the first step of the <b>chain rule</b>, which is used to compute derivatives (weeks 8 and 14).
@@s036 h(x) = (3x − 1)² as f(g(x))
@@s037 Why machine learning is made of compositions
@@s038 In the pipeline of the first lecture each block is a function: cleaning takes a raw datum and returns a clean one, the computation of features takes a datum and returns a vector, the model takes a vector and returns a prediction. The final prediction is therefore a <b>composed function</b>: <span class="m">ŷ = f(Φ(n(x)))</span>.
@@s039 Two practical consequences. First: each piece of the chain must be applied <b>identically</b> in training and in prediction, otherwise the model receives inputs different from those it learned on. Second: to train a chain of functions with gradient descent you must differentiate a composed function, and the tool is the chain rule. A neural network is exactly a long composition of simple functions (<a href="w14-reti-neurali.html">week 14</a>).
@@s040 Normalise, compute features, apply the weights
